Life Cycle of a Thread in Java Multithreading
_____________________________________________________
-in java thread goes through different state from its creation to its termination
-to manage the thread in multithreading, we need to understand the life cuycle of thread
-main states:
a thread goes through 5 main states.

👉 First state – New / Born state

So first we create a thread object.
Like:
Test t = new Test();
So here, thread is created.
You can say the thread is born, but it has not started yet.

👉 Second state – Runnable state
we call:
t.start();
So after calling start(), the thread goes into runnable state.
It is ready to run
But it is waiting for CPU
It depends on JVM and thread scheduler

👉 Third state – Running state
Now what happens, JVM will check and allocate CPU.
Thread scheduler(part of JVM) decides which thread will run based on algorithm like scheduling
Once CPU is allocated → thread goes into running state
Now it starts executing the run() method

👉 Fourth state- Waiting / Blocked / Timed Waiting( Non-Runnable state)
Sometimes what happens, thread is running but we stop it for some time.
This can happen in cases like:
sleep() → thread goes to sleep
waiting for lock
waiting for another thread (join())
suspend (temporarily stop)
So here:
Thread is not running
It is in non-runnable state
After some time or condition, it again goes back to runnable state

👉 Fifth state – Dead state
Now when thread completes its task:
It goes into dead state
That means execution is finished
👉 Important:
Once thread is dead → it cannot come back again
🔄 Flow (same explanation)

👉
New → Runnable → Running → Non-Runnable → Runnable → Dead
"So overall, a thread moves from creation to execution, may pause in between,
 and finally terminates after completing its task."